# tolken -- the Apple Silicon workstation, and the fleet's `mbp`. The two names
# are deliberately different: `mbp` is boot/machines.sh's name for the machine
# and resolves to MACH_SSH=localhost, because that driver is MACH_LOCAL and
# answers only for the computer it runs on. This stanza is how *another* device
# reaches the same hardware, which is what the macOS benchmark lane needs -- the
# build, the staging and the run all happen on tolken, driven from wherever the
# terminal is.
#
# The tailnet name and no HostName, for the same reason the bridges have none:
# the Mac is on the house LAN with no reservation, and it is the one machine in
# the fleet that reboots into a *different macOS install* on purpose. MagicDNS
# follows it across a lease and across that reboot.
#
# The host key is pinned normally. Unlike the rpi4, both installs on this
# machine are long-lived and personalised, so each has one stable key -- two
# known-hosts entries for one name, added once. An unexpected key here is worth
# looking at.
#
# ServerAliveInterval: a bench run is driven over this connection and is
# legitimately silent for minutes at a time, and the session has to outlive
# that. It must NOT outlive the reboot into bench mode, which is why the
# orchestrator reconnects rather than holding one session across it.
# User: `justinmichaud`, and it is the odd one out in this file -- every other
# machine in the fleet is `jmichaud`. That is the account this Mac's *current*
# host install happens to have been created with, not a decision, and it is
# scheduled to be corrected the next time this install is replaced:
# docs/HANDOFF-reprovision.md carries the change so it is not rediscovered
# from a failed login. Do not "tidy" it to jmichaud before then -- the login
# would break, and the failure (`Permission denied (publickey)`) reads exactly
# like an unauthorised key, which is the wrong thing to go looking at.
Host tolken
  User justinmichaud
  ServerAliveInterval 30
  ServerAliveCountMax 6

# The same Mac, in the other mode. One address, two macOS installs, and
# therefore two host keys for one name -- which without this stanza is not a
# nuisance but a hard mid-run failure: ssh refuses a *changed* key outright
# (`StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new` accepts an unknown host and still refuses
# a changed one), so the lane would arm the machine, wait for it to come back,
# and then be unable to talk to what came back.
#
# HostKeyAlias is what that is for. The two installs get separate identities in
# known_hosts while sharing the address, so each is pinned normally and neither
# can masquerade as the other. Putting the bench install on a name of its own
# also makes the mode explicit at the call site: `ssh tolken` is host mode and
# `ssh tolken-bench` is bench mode, and a command aimed at the wrong one fails
# on the key rather than doing the wrong thing quietly.
#
# HostName rather than an address for the same reason as the plain entry: the
# Mac has no DHCP reservation, and the tailnet name follows it. Note the
# tailnet identity belongs to the *machine*, not the install -- the bench
# install has its own tailscale state or none at all, so if it is not on the
# tailnet, set WK_MAC_BENCH_SSH to a .local or LAN name for it instead.
# User: `bench`, and deliberately not a person's name -- not the host install's
# `justinmichaud`, and not the fleet's `jmichaud` either.
#
# A benchmark install is cattle (docs/HANDOFF-cattle.md): it is meant to be
# reproducible from nothing, by anyone, on any machine. Whose laptop it happens
# to be attached to is not a property of the measurement, so an operator's
# account name inside the artifact is exactly the hidden state that document
# forbids -- it makes the rebuild recipe depend on who is running it, which is
# a dependency only discovered by somebody else trying to follow the runbook.
#
# `bench` rather than `user`: `user` already means something specific in this
# file -- pmbootstrap's default account on the tailnet bridges -- and a second
# meaning for it on a different kind of machine is how a stanza gets copied to
# the wrong place. `bench` says what the install is for.
#
# Same reasoning as the Pi bench images logging in as `root` rather than as
# anybody: an impersonal account is the point, and macOS just has no usable
# root login to be the impersonal one.
# HostName is deliberately NOT `tolken`, and this is the correction to an error
# that cost most of an evening on 2026-08-22.
#
# `tolken` resolves through MagicDNS to the tailnet address of the *host*
# install. The bench install is a different operating system with its own
# network stack and no tailscale, so it has no tailnet identity at all -- that
# address reaches host mode or nothing, never bench mode. Every "the bench
# volume is unreachable" that evening was this: ssh, authorized_keys, Remote
# Login and the network were all working, and the connection was being aimed at
# the wrong machine. The paragraph above predicted it in as many words, was
# written before the volume existed, and was not read again until the volume was
# found by scanning the LAN for it.
#
# So: no HostName here, and the address comes from the machine-local
# config.d/local (or WK_MAC_BENCH_SSH), because the bench install's address is
# exactly the kind of thing this repository cannot know -- a DHCP lease on
# somebody's LAN. The proper fix is to give that install its own tailnet
# identity, at which point `HostName tolken-bench` becomes true and belongs
# here; until then a hand-written stanza in config.d/local is the honest
# arrangement, and a missing one fails to resolve instead of silently
# succeeding against the wrong computer.
Host tolken-bench
  HostKeyAlias tolken-bench
  User bench
  ServerAliveInterval 30
  ServerAliveCountMax 6

# The rpi4 test board. Named rpi4-test rather than rpi4 because the fleet name
# is boot/machines.sh's, not ssh's -- `wk boot rpi4` resolves the machine and
# reaches for MACH_SSH, and keeping the two namespaces apart is what makes a
# hand-written `Host rpi4` a mistake ./setup can detect rather than a plausible
# alternative spelling.
#
# Reached through the tailnet bridge, at the address that bridge reserves for
# it -- not by mDNS on the house LAN, which is what this said until
# 2026-08-22. The board sits on the cable behind `tailnet-bridge-generic`
# (that segment is the reason that bridge exists), so `raspberrypi4-64.local`
# resolves nowhere from here: the phone is the only thing on the board's L2,
# and mDNS does not cross it. The board looked simply absent -- `wk boot rpi4
# --status` said "unreachable ... a plain outage" about a board that was up,
# linked and one hop away.
#
# The address is safe to write down for the same reason `moosebmc`'s is:
# bridge/hosts/tailnet-bridge-generic.conf pins it by MAC (BR_LEASES) against
# the MAC boot/machines/rpi4.conf declares, and that MAC is the one thing the
# board keeps across every image it boots. So 10.99.1.10 survives a reflash,
# which .local never did.
#
# ProxyJump rather than a route: reaching 10.99.1.0/24 directly needs
# `--accept-routes`, which is off here. Jumping through the phone needs nothing
# turned on anywhere -- the same choice `moosebmc` makes below.
#
# The *address* is a Host pattern too, and that is load-bearing rather than
# tidy. `wk boot`'s bench-mode channel (`i_ssh`, boot/machines.sh) reaches a
# booted system by bare address with no jump host of its own; a stanza matching
# the literal address is what gives it one. A bench system on this board lands
# on 10.99.1.10 as well -- same NIC, same MAC, same reservation -- so one entry
# covers both modes. The user differs and is meant to: `Host rpi4-test` logs in
# as root, while `i_ssh`'s explicit `<you>@10.99.1.10` wins over the User below,
# which is what that channel wants.
#
# If the cable ever goes back to the house switch, `WK_RPI4_SSH` overrides
# MACH_SSH and `raspberrypi4-64.local` is the name to point it at.
#
# The host key is deliberately not pinned. The whole point of this board is
# that it boots a different image on demand, and each one brings its own key.
#
# LogLevel goes with that and is not cosmetic. With the known-hosts file at
# /dev/null, ssh "permanently adds" the key on *every* connection and says so,
# which put seven identical warnings into one `wk sysimage write` -- and a
# warning that fires unconditionally is worse than silence, because it trains
# the eye to skip the place real warnings appear. ERROR, not QUIET: a failure
# to connect still has to be audible.
Host rpi4-test 10.99.1.10
  HostName 10.99.1.10
  User root
  ProxyJump tailnet-bridge-generic
  StrictHostKeyChecking no
  UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null
  LogLevel ERROR

# The rpi3 test board. `Host rpi3` and not `rpi3-test`: unlike the rpi4, no
# shared build box has ever answered to this name, so there is no collision to
# keep the namespaces apart from -- and MACH_SSH in boot/machines/rpi3.conf
# already spells it `rpi3`, which is the name this has to match.
#
# HostName is the mDNS name, not an address, and that is the load-bearing part.
# This board is on the house LAN with no reservation anyone here controls, and
# it was seen holding *two* DHCP leases at once on one interface (192.168.1.169
# and .171, both live on eth0) -- the same firmware-and-kernel-disagree-about-
# the-client-identifier behaviour the rpi4 conf describes. An address written
# here would be right until the next lease. The image runs avahi and announces
# `raspberrypi3`, and moose resolves it through nss-mdns (`mdns4_minimal` in
# /etc/nsswitch.conf), so the name is the stable half.
#
# No ProxyJump: this board is cabled to the house LAN directly, not onto the
# phone's segment. bridge/hosts/tailnet-bridge-generic.conf still calls
# 10.99.1.0/24 "the rpi3/rpi4 cable" -- only the rpi4 has ever appeared there.
#
# The host key is deliberately not pinned, for the rpi4's reason: the whole
# point of this board is that it boots a different image on demand and each one
# brings its own key. LogLevel ERROR goes with that -- with known-hosts at
# /dev/null ssh announces a permanently-added key on every single connection,
# and a warning that fires unconditionally trains the eye to skip the place
# real warnings appear.
Host rpi3
  HostName raspberrypi3.local
  User root
  StrictHostKeyChecking no
  UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null
  LogLevel ERROR

# The tailnet bridges: phones routing a segment that cannot join the tailnet
# itself (moose's BMC port, the rpi3/rpi4 cable). `wk bridge` drives them over
# these entries -- see `wk help bridge`.
#
# The name is the tailnet name and there is no HostName: the whole point of a
# bridge is to be reachable when the LAN is not the way in, so resolving it
# through MagicDNS rather than an address is the property worth keeping. It also
# means these entries work unchanged from anywhere on the tailnet.
#
# ServerAliveInterval because both phones roam between two APs on one SSID, and
# a roam stalls TCP long enough to kill an idle session while the phone itself
# never wavered. That symptom is a keepalive's problem, not a fault to chase.
#
# The host key is deliberately *not* pinned away like the rpi4's: root on a
# bridge is equivalent to whatever is behind it, so an unexpected key is
# something to look at rather than something to shrug past. A reflash does
# change it -- remove the old line from known_hosts when that is what happened.
#
# User: pmbootstrap's default account, and what a pmOS release image's first-boot
# wizard suggests. Password auth is off on the phone (bridge/provision.sh), so
# this is a key login or nothing.
# The `.local` spellings are here for one moment: the first run against a
# freshly flashed phone, which has no tailnet name yet because `wk bridge setup`
# is what gives it one. The image carries avahi for exactly that, and cmd/bridge
# falls back to this name on its own.
# moose's BMC, reached through the Librem 5 that fronts its management port.
#
# Repo-owned rather than machine-local, because the address is not a local
# preference: bridge/hosts/tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.conf pins it by MAC
# (BR_LEASES) and makes it the entire DHCP pool, so 10.99.0.2 is the only
# address the BMC can have. A hand-written copy of this stanza is how it went
# stale -- it pointed at 192.168.1.41, which answers nothing, and
# config.d/local is read *before* this file, so the stale copy won every time
# it was corrected here. host/dotfiles.sh now drops hand-written stanzas for
# names this file defines, for that reason.
#
# ProxyJump rather than a route: 10.99.0.0/24 is advertised by the bridge and
# reaching it needs --accept-routes, which is off. Jumping through the phone
# needs nothing turned on anywhere.
Host moosebmc
  HostName 10.99.0.2
  User root
  Port 2200
  ProxyJump librem5-oob

Host tailnet-bridge-generic tailnet-bridge-generic.local tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.local
  User user
  ServerAliveInterval 15
  ServerAliveCountMax 4
